# Scala 3 end markers, as scalafmt's `rewrite.scala3.endMarker.insert`
# with sbt's `breaks.min = 20`: a statement that owns a region of
# significant indentation gets `end name` when its last block prints over
# 20 line breaks or more, counting from the line the block opens on, as
# with scalafmt's `spanHas = lastBlockOnly`: the marker closes it as a `}`
# would. Only where the statement is in a template, the file, or a block
# of more than one statement. A marker already there is the printer's to
# keep or drop by the same rule.

<<< 20 line breaks get a marker, 19 don't
object A {
  def long =
    a0()
    a1()
    a2()
    a3()
    a4()
    a5()
    a6()
    a7()
    a8()
    a9()
    a10()
    a11()
    a12()
    a13()
    a14()
    a15()
    a16()
    a17()
    a18()
    a19()
  def short =
    b0()
    b1()
    b2()
    b3()
    b4()
    b5()
    b6()
    b7()
    b8()
    b9()
    b10()
    b11()
    b12()
    b13()
    b14()
    b15()
    b16()
    b17()
    b18()
}
>>>
